    oilSpecification: "VW 505.01 5W-40 Fully Synthetic",
    commonIssues: [ 
        "Camshaft and Lifter Wear: The most notorious issue with early PD engines. The narrow cam lobes actuate both the valves and the 2000-bar injectors. Using non-505.01 oil or delaying oil changes causes the cam lobes to wear flat and punch through the hydraulic lifters, causing a loud 'chuffing' noise from the intake.",
        "Injector Wiring Harness Failure: The wiring loom to the unit injectors sits inside the hot, oily cylinder head. The wires become brittle and short out, causing the engine to drop a cylinder and shake violently.",
        "Tandem Pump Leaks: The tandem pump (vacuum/fuel pump) mounted on the back of the cylinder head frequently leaks diesel externally onto coolant hoses, or internally, diluting the engine oil with diesel fuel.",
        "Sticky VNT Turbo Vanes: Soot builds up in the variable geometry mechanism, causing the car to go into 'Limp Mode' due to overboost.",
        "Head Gasket Failure: The extreme cylinder pressures of the PD engine can sometimes lift the cylinder head and blow the gasket, especially if tuned aggressively.",
        "EGR Valve & Intake Clogging: Massive carbon-tar buildup restricts intake airflow."
    ],
    vulnerabilities: [ 
        "Catastrophic Camshaft Failure - If cheap oil is used, the camshaft will be destroyed. Replacing the camshaft, lifters, and bearings is a major expense.",
        "Engine Bearing Failure (Diesel in Oil) - If the tandem pump leaks internally, diesel thins out the engine oil, leading to spun rod bearings.",
        "Valvetrain Destruction (Timing Belt Snap) - The timing belt on a PD is under immense strain. If the 90k km interval is ignored, it will snap and destroy the engine.",
        "Dual Mass Flywheel (DMF) Failure - The massive 285 Nm torque spike at 1900 RPM tortures the DMF. It will eventually clatter and vibrate violently, requiring replacement."
    ],
